    "lawCode": "Add §224-c, amd Art 11 Head & §212, Pub Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Public Service Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5696\n\nSPONSOR: METZGER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public service law, in relation to\nthe public service commission reviewing broadband and fiber optic\nservices within the state\n\n \nPURPOSE::\n\nThe purpose of this legislation is to identify areas of the state will\ninsufficient broadband access and to set targets for the completion of\ninstallation of broadband infrastructure.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS::\n\nSection 1 - Legislative Findings - The Legislature finds that large\nportions of the state do not have access to broadband and reliable\ninternet service. Insufficient access to broadband is a burden to\nschools, families, businesses, and other vital services.\n\nSection 2 - Creates a new section 224-c of the public service law,\ndirecting the public service commission to identify areas in need of\nbroadband and fiber optic service. The Commission must also require and\nset completion dates for the construction or installation of broadband\nand fiber optic services.\n\nSection 3 - Re-titles Article 11 as \"Provisions Relating to Cable Tele-\nvision Companies and Internet Service Providers\".\n\nSection 4 - Amends section 212 of the public service law to define\ninternet service provider. Section 5- Effective date - 60th day.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION::\n\nMany areas of the state do not have access to or have insufficient\naccess to broadband services, in particular fiber optic service. A\nrecent study found that 8.7 million New Yorkers are not accessing the\ninternet at high speed rates. Without access to high speed internet New\nYork families are unable to access an essential service.  Without\naccess, families and businesses are being left behind as the technolog-\nical world advances. In order for communities, in particular rural\ncommunities to be successful it is imperative that access to high speed\nbe available and affordable to all New Yorkers.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY::\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS::\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE::\n\nThis act shall take effect one year after it shall have become a law.\n\n\n\n\n",
